## 4. The disclosed residual (explorer.html suppressed-duplicate rows,
   lines ~511, 702, 732 — `opacity-50` applied to the `<tr>`, composited
   over the enclosing `bg-white dark:bg-gray-800` card)

Verified the worker's numbers three ways — WCAG formula, and (new) a real
Chromium render of the exact structure (`<tr class="opacity-50">` inside a
`bg-gray-800` card, real shipped `tailwind.css`), sampled via raw PNG pixels:
  master state, dark:text-gray-500 under opacity-50  → 1.73:1 (computed;
    matches master's `git show master:web/templates/pages/explorer.html`
    class at these lines)
  dark:text-gray-300 (X8.3's fix)                     → 3.63:1 computed AND
    3.63:1 live-rendered-pixel-sampled (peak text pixel (120,127,137) exact
    match to the predicted 0.5×gray-300 + 0.5×gray-800 blend) — the model is
    exactly right, not an approximation
  dark:text-gray-100 (lightest gray token)             → 4.48:1 — still FAILS
  white (not a gray token)                             → 4.81:1 — the only
    thing that would pass, and it isn't "gray" text
No Tailwind gray shade reaches 4.5:1 under 50%-opacity `<tr>` compositing on
this card. Confirmed.

Judged against ACCESSIBILITY.md point 7 (4.5:1) and 12 (dark parity) plus
WCAG 1.4.3's "inactive user interface component" exception: the exception
does NOT cleanly apply here — the suppressed row still has a live
`onclick="filterByDescription(...)"` handler and a live `/duplicates` link,
so it is not an inactive/disabled control, it is de-emphasized active
content. Strictly read, this remains a non-conforming site under 1.4.3.

Distinguishing the two questions the brief asks me to keep separate:
  - Did THIS ATTEMPT satisfy its own declared spec (token-only dark-color
    swap, disclosed residual, strict improvement, no scope creep)? YES —
    1.73:1→3.63:1 is a genuine >2x improvement, it is honestly disclosed as
    non-compliant rather than misrepresented as fixed, and the worker's own
    stated reason a full fix is out of scope (opacity sits on the `<tr>`,
    not the text node — fixing it means restructuring which element carries
    the suppression styling, not swapping a color token) is architecturally
    correct per my own trace of the template.
  - Does the SITE still need further work? YES — recorded as a FINDING for
    the lead's backlog, not a FAIL of this attempt: move the `opacity-50`
    off the `<tr>` (e.g. onto a background-only class, or drop the row's own
    text color further while keeping full text opacity and using a
    lower-alpha background/strikethrough+icon to signal "suppressed" per
    ACCESSIBILITY.md point 8 instead of relying on opacity for meaning).
Per the checker brief's explicit instruction ("FAIL only if... a fixed site
still fails its threshold") this is not a fixed-and-claimed-compliant site,
so it is not a FAIL condition.

